# Vamsa 🌳

[![CI](https://github.com/rmbhia1/vamsa/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/rmbhia1/vamsa/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Node Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D20-brightgreen)](https://nodejs.org)

A command-line family tree management tool with local-first storage. Vamsa (वंश) means "lineage" or "family line" in Sanskrit.

## Features

- **Local-First Storage**: Your family data stays on your machine in human-readable JSON
- **Privacy-Focused**: No cloud sync, no accounts, complete data ownership
- **Rich Querying**: Find relatives by relationship, generation, or custom criteria
- **Multiple Export Formats**: GEDCOM, JSON, Markdown, and Mermaid diagrams
- **Data Validation**: Ensures logical consistency (birth before death, valid relationships)
- **Backup & Restore**: Simple backup management with timestamps

## Requirements

- Node.js >= 20
- npm >= 10

## Installation

### From npm (recommended)

```bash
npm install -g vamsa
```

### From source

```bash
git clone https://github.com/rmbhia1/vamsa.git
cd vamsa
npm install
npm run build
npm link
```

## Quick Start

```bash
# Initialize a new family tree
vamsa init "The Smith Family"

# Add the first person
vamsa add "John Smith" --birth-date 1950-03-15 --gender male

# Add a spouse
vamsa add "Jane Smith" --birth-date 1952-07-22 --gender female

# Create the marriage relationship
vamsa edit <john-id> --add-spouse <jane-id>

# Add a child
vamsa add "Tom Smith" --birth-date 1975-01-10 --gender male --father <john-id> --mother <jane-id>

# View the tree
vamsa tree

# Show person details
vamsa show <person-id>

# Find relatives
vamsa find siblings <tom-id>
vamsa find cousins <tom-id>
```

## Commands

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `vamsa init <name>` | Create a new family tree |
| `vamsa add <name>` | Add a new person |
| `vamsa edit <id>` | Edit a person's details |
| `vamsa delete <id>` | Remove a person |
| `vamsa show <id>` | Display person details |
| `vamsa list` | List all people |
| `vamsa tree` | Display visual family tree |
| `vamsa find <relation> <id>` | Find relatives |
| `vamsa stats` | Show tree statistics |
| `vamsa export` | Export to various formats |
| `vamsa import <file>` | Import from GEDCOM/JSON |
| `vamsa validate` | Check data integrity |
| `vamsa backup` | Create a backup |
| `vamsa restore <file>` | Restore from backup |
| `vamsa path` | Show data file location |

## Data Storage

Vamsa stores data in `~/.vamsa/family-tree.json` by default. The format is human-readable JSON:

```json
{
  "meta": {
    "name": "The Smith Family",
    "created": "2024-01-15T10:30:00Z",
    "modified": "2024-01-15T10:30:00Z",
    "version": "1.0.0"
  },
  "people": {
    "abc123": {
      "id": "abc123",
      "name": { "first": "John", "last": "Smith" },
      "birth": { "date": "1950-03-15" },
      "gender": "male"
    }
  },
  "relationships": []
}
```

## Documentation

- [Development Guide](docs/DEVELOPMENT.md)
- [Contributing](docs/CONTRIBUTING.md)
- [File Format Specification](docs/FILE_FORMAT.md)

## Development

```bash
# Install dependencies
npm install

# Run in development mode
npm run dev

# Run tests
npm test

# Lint code
npm run lint

# Type check
npm run typecheck

# Build
npm run build
```

